# SRG-APP-000340-WSR-000029  -  CAT II
# Non-privileged accounts on the hosting system must only access web server
# security-relevant information and functions through a distinct
# administrative account.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000340-WSR-000029
group:           V-206419
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-002235
control_rev5:    AC-6

contribution:    Splunk's own role-based access control is where the separation is
                 expressed: capabilities are disabled by default and each authorize.conf
                 [role_<roleName>] stanza grants them explicitly, so the product can hold a
                 distinct administrative role whose security-relevant capabilities no
                 non-privileged role inherits.

external:        The SRG scopes this to non-privileged accounts on the hosting system:
                 operating system accounts, their group membership, and their access to the
                 installation directory and configuration files are carried by the
                 operating system.
external:        Which Splunk roles and accounts count as privileged is an organizational
                 determination the application cannot infer from any value.
external:        Where authentication is delegated, the directory service or identity
                 provider decides group membership and therefore which role a user
                 receives.
external:        An assessor reviews the capability grants in each authorize.conf
                 [role_<roleName>] stanza against the organization's list of administrative
                 and non-privileged roles.

manual_reason:   Separation depends on which capabilities each role carries and which
                 accounts hold which role, and the vocabulary cannot name "the
                 non-privileged roles" without the organization's own role assignment; the
                 SRG's subject is also accounts on the hosting system.

citation:        importRoles present and grantableRoles present test nothing about
                 separation and fail compliant roles. A role is defined by its capability
                 lines - "<capability> = <enabled>", and "'enabled' is the only accepted
                 value here, as capabilities are disabled by default" - while importRoles
                 defaults to "A role imports no other roles" (authorize.conf.spec:67-84),
                 so a non-privileged role that imports nothing is exactly what this
                 requirement wants and a present test reports it as a finding.
                 grantableRoles is narrower still: it bounds what a user holding
                 "edit_roles_grantable" and "edit_user" may manage
                 (authorize.conf.spec:86-98), so requiring it of every role stanza is a
                 finding against every role that delegates no user management, which is
                 most of them. Neither key says whether a non-privileged account can reach
                 security-relevant information or functions; that is decided by the
                 capability set of each role and by the organization's designation of which
                 roles and accounts are privileged, neither of which this vocabulary can
                 express over a generic [role_<roleName>] stanza. The SRG's subject is also
                 "non-privileged accounts on the hosting system", which is the operating
                 system's account base, so the larger part of this objective was never the
                 application's to carry.
finding:         Non-privileged Splunk roles hold capabilities that reach security-relevant
                 information or functions, instead of those capabilities being confined to
                 a distinct administrative role in authorize.conf.
